#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749 :topic/difficulty 0.6467073363985405 :topic/id #uuid "bad8d582-b827-501b-8a06-58b93e5ecdb0" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096610 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096410 :question/id #uuid "8e4367b2-f7bc-5588-b30b-d612915e3e68" :question/math-academy-id "e-3624" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=3e^x + \\ln 3$, find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of $e^x$ is just $e^x$ itself. On the other hand, $\\ln 3$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3e^x + \\ln 3\\right)\\\n                                                  &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( 3e^x\\right) +  \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( \\ln 3 \\right)\\\n                                                  &= 3\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(e^x\\right) + 0\\\n                                                  &= 3 e^x.\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096611 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096411 :question/id #uuid "48334c0c-a49e-5dfb-9f8a-8caa0c22d1f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3625" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=4e^x+ 3x^4 - x^2,$ find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiate each term using the constant multiple, exponential, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x)\n&= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(4e^x+3x^4-x^2\\right) \\\\\n&= 4\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x)\n + 3\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(x^4)\n - \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(x^2) \\\\\n&= 4e^x+3(4x^3)-2x \\\\\n&= 4e^x+12x^3-2x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b7d0153-6e0a-5e19-83f1-960811dc5205" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Polynomial"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096612 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096412 :question/id #uuid "16b1a91c-ae83-5e6b-a11d-bb9d78e4d218" :question/math-academy-id "e-3626" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=3 \\cdot 2^x$, find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf'(x)      &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(3 \\cdot 2^x\\right) \\\n                            &= 3 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(2^x\\right) \\\n                            &=  3 \\cdot 2^{x}\\ln(2) \\\n                            &=  3 \\ln(2) \\cdot 2^{x}.\n\t}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14e68965-6c39-5947-ac05-eee99e685063" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Base Other Than e"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1114 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :topic/title "Differentiating Exponential Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690 :topic/difficulty 0.8054939588520751 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428108 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1116->278. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} :encompassing/weight 1.0}] :topic/id #uuid "5f59a4ad-6d31-5757-be65-f24e070b2c60" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096613 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096413 :question/id #uuid "6624681a-bb00-59db-8df9-4d5a3cd00434" :question/math-academy-id "e-3627" :question/problem "Given that $y=5\\ln x$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\ln x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}   & = \\left(5\\ln x\\right)'\\  \n                                                    & = 5 (\\ln x)'\\\n                                                    &=5\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x}\\\n                                                    &= \\dfrac{5}{x}.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t  \n\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a71301e9-bc7f-53b9-900e-c3bd37f96b03" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Natural Logarithm Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096614 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096414 :question/id #uuid "58d47fb0-cf75-58c4-8eea-f3ae97333159" :question/math-academy-id "e-3628" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2\\ln x+x^2-1$, find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\tf'(x) &= \\left(2\\ln x+x^2-1\\right)'\\\n\t\t\t&=2 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x}+2x-0\\\n\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{2}{x}+2x.\n\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83ce3c28-857b-5538-b362-cf52526e3688" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum Containing a Natural Logarithm Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096615 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096415 :question/id #uuid "f61ac550-d62c-558f-b6d5-0d4c9aba8089" :question/math-academy-id "e-3629" :question/problem "Given that $y=4\\log_3 x,$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply our formula for the derivative when the base of the logarithm is a number other than $e.$ By doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4\\log_3 x\\right)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t &=4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\log_3 x\\right)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t &=4\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x \\ln 3}\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{4}{x \\ln 3}.\n\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5856f3cc-5423-5a97-b2fd-7f44e0797ec7" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Logarithmic Function With a General Base"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096616 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096416 :question/id #uuid "5eaa00f8-0fd0-5de6-a7c0-965db6de3a5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3630" :question/problem "Given that $y= \\log_4 x + \\ln x$,  find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( \\log_4 x + \\ln x \\right)\\\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\log_4 x) + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\ln x )\\\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln 4} + \\dfrac{1}{x}.\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{1}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cef7fc21-23f4-5d6a-a208-9963ef23c784" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent Line of a Logarithmic Function at a Point"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1116 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050902}] :topic/title "Differentiating Logarithmic Functions"}]}
